# All six group-split models, in dependency order.
#
#     condor_submit_dag oco2.dag
#     condor_q -dag
#     tail -f oco2.dag.dagman.out
#
# Shape of the graph:
#
#                              prep
#                (split table + training-only statistics)
#                                |
#           +---------+----------+----------+-----------+
#           |         |          |          |
#       det_xco2  det_profiles  det_last4  nf_xco2        <- run concurrently
#                     |          |
#                 nf_profiles  nf_last4                   <- warm start from the backbone
#
# Four GPUs at once in the middle layer, then two. On a quiet day that is roughly the
# wall time of the slowest single model plus the slowest flow, rather than the sum of all
# six, which is the entire reason for moving off a two-GPU box.
#
# DAGMan restarts from a rescue file, so if one node fails you fix it and re-run the same
# command -- the completed nodes are not repeated:
#     condor_submit_dag oco2.dag          # picks up oco2.dag.rescue001 automatically

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nodes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
JOB prep prep.sub

JOB det_xco2     train.sub
JOB det_profiles train.sub
JOB det_last4    train.sub
JOB nf_xco2      train.sub

JOB nf_profiles  train_dep.sub
JOB nf_last4     train_dep.sub

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-node variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# gpu_length is CHTC's GPU Lab duration class: "short" <= 12 h, "medium" <= 24 h,
# "long" <= 7 days. Shorter classes match more slots and start sooner, so ask for the
# smallest one the run fits in. The epoch counts come from the *_grouped.yaml configs:
# 25 for the scalar regressor, 40 for the profile regressors, 35-50 for the flows, and
# 100 for the near-surface flow -- which is the only one that needs "long".

VARS det_xco2     job_name="det_xco2"     gpu_length="short"
VARS det_profiles job_name="det_profiles" gpu_length="medium"
VARS det_last4    job_name="det_last4"    gpu_length="medium"
VARS nf_xco2      job_name="nf_xco2"      gpu_length="medium"

VARS nf_profiles  job_name="nf_profiles"  gpu_length="medium" dep_tar="det_profiles.tar.gz"
# nf_last4 was "long" for 100 epochs. The checkpoint audit put it back to the published
# 50 (see nf_profiles4_grouped.yaml), so "medium" fits and will match a slot sooner.
VARS nf_last4     job_name="nf_last4"     gpu_length="medium" dep_tar="det_last4.tar.gz"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Edges
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PARENT prep         CHILD det_xco2 det_profiles det_last4 nf_xco2

# nf_profiles_grouped.yaml sets
#   setup.backbone_ckpt: .../group_split_v1/full_regressor_profiles/net_profiles.pt
# and nf_profiles4_grouped.yaml sets
#   model.pretrained_ckpt: .../group_split_v1/full_regressor_profiles_last4/net_profiles.pt
# Both are produced by their parent node here. nf_xco2 has backbone_ckpt: null and so
# hangs off prep directly.
PARENT det_profiles CHILD nf_profiles
PARENT det_last4    CHILD nf_last4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Throttles
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here is large by CHTC standards, but keeping the GPU footprint modest keeps us
# in the good graces of the shared GPU Lab pool.
MAXJOBS GPU 4
CATEGORY det_xco2     GPU
CATEGORY det_profiles GPU
CATEGORY det_last4    GPU
CATEGORY nf_xco2      GPU
CATEGORY nf_profiles  GPU
CATEGORY nf_last4     GPU

# A machine-readable snapshot of node states, refreshed every 30 s. Handy when you are
# checking on a run from your phone:  grep -A2 'Node ' oco2.dag.status
#
# DAGMan already keeps going after a node fails -- it only stops descending into that
# node's children -- and writes oco2.dag.rescue001 listing what is left. So a failure in,
# say, det_last4 still lets the other three deterministic models and nf_profiles finish.
NODE_STATUS_FILE oco2.dag.status 30
